module

public import TauCeti.GroupTheory.SpecificGroups.Dihedral.Basic
public import TauCeti.GroupTheory.TrivialIntersection

/-!
# The odd dihedral groups of degree `n > 1` are Frobenius groups

At every degree `n ≠ 0`, `DihedralGroup n` is the semidirect product `C_n ⋊ C₂` of its rotation
subgroup by the two-element subgroup generated by any one reflection. What oddness of `n` adds is
that the reflection subgroup then meets each of its distinct conjugates trivially; that and its
nontriviality hold at every odd `n`, including `n = 1`. Once `n > 1` the reflection subgroup is in
addition proper, and the decomposition exhibits `DihedralGroup n` as a **Frobenius group**. The
excluded degree `n = 1` is the two-element group, where the reflection subgroup is the whole group.

The two halves of the decomposition are already in place. The rotation subgroup
`TauCeti.dihedralRotations` is normal of order `n` and the subgroup generated by a reflection is
`{1, sr i}` of order `2` (`TauCeti.mem_zpowers_sr_iff`); those orders multiply to the order `2 * n`
of the group and the two subgroups meet only in the identity, so they are complementary at every
`n ≠ 0` -- `D₄ = C₄ ⋊ C₂` just as much as `D₃ = C₃ ⋊ C₂`. Oddness enters only in the second half.
The trivial-intersection property is the fixed-point-free criterion
`TauCeti.isTISubgroup_of_isComplement'_of_fixedPointFree`, whose hypothesis is the defining
dihedral relation: conjugation by a reflection inverts every rotation
(`TauCeti.conjNormal_eq_inv_of_notMem_dihedralRotations`), and a rotation equal to its own inverse
has order dividing `2`, hence order `1` when `n` is odd.

**Oddness is load-bearing there, not cosmetic.** For even `n > 0` the rotation `r (n/2)` is central
and is neither the identity nor a reflection, so it normalizes the reflection subgroup while lying
outside it, and the trivial-intersection property fails;
`TauCeti.not_isTISubgroup_zpowers_sr` records that failure at every such degree. That is a
statement about the *finite* dihedral groups only. At the remaining even degree `n = 0` the
rotation `r (n/2)` is the identity, and the reflection subgroup of the infinite dihedral group
really is a trivial-intersection subgroup: conjugating `sr i` by `r k` gives `sr (i - 2k)` and by
`sr j` gives `sr (2j - i)`, and over `ZMod 0 = ℤ` neither returns `sr i` unless the conjugator
already lies in the subgroup. The Frobenius *kernel* of the complement built here is identified
with the rotation subgroup in `TauCeti/RepresentationTheory/FrobeniusGroup/Dihedral.lean`, where
the character theory behind Frobenius's theorem is available.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.isComplement'_dihedralRotations_zpowers_sr`: at every degree `n ≠ 0`, the rotation
  subgroup and the subgroup generated by a reflection are complementary, so
  `DihedralGroup n = C_n ⋊ C₂`.
* `TauCeti.fixedPointFree_conjNormal_dihedralRotations`: for odd `n`, conjugation by a reflection
  fixes no rotation but the identity.
* `TauCeti.isTISubgroup_zpowers_sr`: for odd `n`, the subgroup generated by a reflection is a
  trivial-intersection subgroup.
* `TauCeti.isFrobeniusComplement_zpowers_sr`: **for odd `n > 1` it is a Frobenius complement**, so
  `DihedralGroup n` is a Frobenius group.
* `TauCeti.not_isTISubgroup_zpowers_sr`: the negative control, at every positive even degree.

## References

* I. M. Isaacs, *Character Theory of Finite Groups*, AMS Chelsea (1976), Chapter 7, where the
  dihedral groups of odd degree are the standard first examples of Frobenius groups.
* J.-P. Serre, *Linear Representations of Finite Groups*, Springer GTM 42 (1977), Chapter 5.3,
  where the dihedral groups appear with the same semidirect decomposition.
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti

variable {n : ℕ}

/-! ### The semidirect decomposition -/

/-- **The rotation subgroup and a reflection subgroup are complementary at every finite degree**,
so `DihedralGroup n = C_n ⋊ C₂`. Their orders `n` and `2` multiply to the order `2 * n` of the
group, and the two subgroups meet only in the identity
(`TauCeti.eq_one_of_mem_dihedralRotations_of_mem_zpowers_sr`). Oddness of `n` plays no part: it is
what the fixed-point-free action below needs, not what makes the two subgroups complementary. -/
theorem isComplement'_dihedralRotations_zpowers_sr [NeZero n] (i : ZMod n) :
    (dihedralRotations n).IsComplement' (Subgroup.zpowers (DihedralGroup.sr i)) :=
  Subgroup.isComplement'_of_card_mul_and_disjoint
    (by rw [card_dihedralRotations, Nat.card_zpowers, DihedralGroup.orderOf_sr,
      DihedralGroup.nat_card, Nat.mul_comm])
    (Subgroup.disjoint_def.2 fun hg hgs =>
      eq_one_of_mem_dihedralRotations_of_mem_zpowers_sr hg hgs)

/-! ### The fixed-point-free action -/

/-- **For odd `n`, conjugation by a reflection fixes no rotation but the identity.** Conjugation by
anything outside the rotation subgroup inverts every rotation, so a fixed rotation is its own
inverse and has order dividing `2`; its order also divides `n`, and `2` is prime to an odd `n`. -/
theorem fixedPointFree_conjNormal_dihedralRotations (hn : Odd n) {s : DihedralGroup n}
    (hs : s ∉ dihedralRotations n) :
    MonoidHom.FixedPointFree (MulAut.conjNormal s : MulAut (dihedralRotations n)) := by
  intro x hx
  rw [conjNormal_eq_inv_of_notMem_dihedralRotations hs] at hx
  have hsq : x ^ 2 = 1 := by
    rw [pow_two]
    nth_rewrite 1 [← hx]
    exact inv_mul_cancel x
  have htwo : orderOf x ∣ 2 := orderOf_dvd_of_pow_eq_one hsq
  have hcard : orderOf x ∣ n := by
    have hdvd := orderOf_dvd_natCard x
    rwa [card_dihedralRotations] at hdvd
  have hcop : Nat.Coprime (orderOf x) n :=
    Nat.Coprime.coprime_dvd_left htwo (Nat.coprime_two_left.mpr hn)
  exact orderOf_eq_one_iff.mp (hcop.eq_one_of_dvd hcard)

/-! ### The Frobenius complement -/

/-- A nonidentity element of the subgroup generated by a reflection lies outside the rotation
subgroup, so `TauCeti.fixedPointFree_conjNormal_dihedralRotations` applies to it. This is the
fixed-point-free hypothesis shared by `TauCeti.isTISubgroup_zpowers_sr` and
`TauCeti.isFrobeniusComplement_zpowers_sr`. -/
private theorem fixedPointFree_conjNormal_of_mem_zpowers_sr (hn : Odd n) {i : ZMod n}
    {h : Subgroup.zpowers (DihedralGroup.sr i)} (hh : h ≠ 1) :
    MonoidHom.FixedPointFree
      (MulAut.conjNormal (h : DihedralGroup n) : MulAut (dihedralRotations n)) :=
  fixedPointFree_conjNormal_dihedralRotations hn fun hmem =>
    hh (Subtype.ext (eq_one_of_mem_dihedralRotations_of_mem_zpowers_sr hmem h.2))

/-- **For odd `n` the subgroup generated by a reflection is a trivial-intersection subgroup.** It
complements the rotation subgroup and acts on it without nonidentity fixed points. -/
theorem isTISubgroup_zpowers_sr (hn : Odd n) (i : ZMod n) :
    IsTISubgroup (Subgroup.zpowers (DihedralGroup.sr i)) := by
  have : NeZero n := ⟨hn.pos.ne'⟩
  exact isTISubgroup_of_isComplement'_of_fixedPointFree
    (isComplement'_dihedralRotations_zpowers_sr i)
    fun _ hh => fixedPointFree_conjNormal_of_mem_zpowers_sr hn hh

/-- **For odd `n > 1`, `DihedralGroup n` is a Frobenius group with complement the subgroup
generated by a reflection.** The excluded degree `n = 1` is the group of order two, which a single
reflection exhausts. -/
theorem isFrobeniusComplement_zpowers_sr (hn : Odd n) (hn1 : n ≠ 1) (i : ZMod n) :
    IsFrobeniusComplement (Subgroup.zpowers (DihedralGroup.sr i)) := by
  have : NeZero n := ⟨hn.pos.ne'⟩
  exact isFrobeniusComplement_of_isComplement'_of_fixedPointFree
    (isComplement'_dihedralRotations_zpowers_sr i)
    (Subgroup.zpowers_ne_bot.mpr (sr_ne_one i)) (zpowers_sr_ne_top hn1 i)
    fun _ hh => fixedPointFree_conjNormal_of_mem_zpowers_sr hn hh

/-! ### The negative control at an even degree -/

/-- **At every even degree `n > 0` the trivial-intersection property fails**, so oddness is what
`TauCeti.isTISubgroup_zpowers_sr` is really using. The rotation `r (n/2)` is central in
`DihedralGroup n`, so conjugating the subgroup generated by a reflection by it returns that
subgroup unchanged. It is itself neither the identity nor a reflection, so it lies outside that
subgroup, and the trivial-intersection condition -- that `g H g⁻¹` meet `H` only in the
identity for every `g ∉ H` -- fails. The hypothesis `n ≠ 0` is not an artefact of the proof: in the
infinite dihedral group the reflection subgroup *is* a trivial-intersection subgroup, so this is a
statement about the finite dihedral groups. -/
theorem not_isTISubgroup_zpowers_sr (hn : Even n) (hn0 : n ≠ 0) (i : ZMod n) :
    ¬ IsTISubgroup (Subgroup.zpowers (DihedralGroup.sr i)) := by
  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := hn
  intro h
  have hmm : (m : ZMod n) + m = 0 := by
    rw [← Nat.cast_add, ← hm, ZMod.natCast_self]
  have hm0 : (m : ZMod n) ≠ 0 := by
    rw [Ne, ZMod.natCast_eq_zero_iff]
    exact fun hdvd => by have := Nat.le_of_dvd (by omega) hdvd; omega
  have hr1 : DihedralGroup.r (m : ZMod n) ≠ 1 := fun hc =>
    hm0 (by rwa [← DihedralGroup.r_zero, DihedralGroup.r.injEq] at hc)
  have hnotmem : DihedralGroup.r (m : ZMod n) ∉ Subgroup.zpowers (DihedralGroup.sr i) := by
    rw [mem_zpowers_sr_iff]
    simp [hr1]
  -- `r m` is central, `m + m` being `0`, so conjugation by it fixes `sr i`.
  have hcentral : DihedralGroup.r (m : ZMod n) * DihedralGroup.sr i
      * (DihedralGroup.r (m : ZMod n))⁻¹ = DihedralGroup.sr i := by
    rw [DihedralGroup.r_mul_sr, DihedralGroup.inv_r, DihedralGroup.sr_mul_r]
    congr 1
    rw [← sub_eq_add_neg, sub_sub, hmm, sub_zero]
  refine sr_ne_one i (h.eq_one hnotmem (Subgroup.mem_zpowers _) ?_)
  rw [hcentral]
  exact Subgroup.mem_zpowers _

end TauCeti
